  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 PM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n===== Frosty Mornings, Cool Afternoons =====\n\nTemperatures have already maxed out into the mid and upper 50s with \nongoing cooling behind an advancing cold front. The front is \ncurrently over Lexington draped SW-NE. Besides some general \nsprinkles, drizzles, and mizzles, this evening will be calm with a \nlight northerly flow reinforcing the cool air tonight. So much so, \nthat there is an active Frost Advisory out, with an 80-100% of frost \nin our SW Indiana counties, lessening to 40-60% in our SE KY \ncounties (and <20% in the Louisville metro). This advisory serves as \na reminder for anyone to bring in any sensitive plants should there \nbe any left outdoors this late in the season. \n\nSkies will begin to clear from NW-SE this evening near 8 PM EST into \nthe overnight across our southeastern communities. With that, \ntemperatures will cool easily into the 30s by Saturday morning. \nPatchy fog may develop in some of the lowest lying areas in our \nwest, though chances remain low near 20%. Morning frost will melt \naway in the mid morning hours with mostly sunny skies and highs in \nthe low to mid 50s. Northwesterly flow aloft will ensure we stay \ncool tomorrow and with clear skies and light to calm winds, \ntemperatures tumble into the upper 20s and low 30s tomorrow night. \nThis widespread sub-freezing temperature signal means that there \nwill likely be a freeze product issued for then. Patchy valley fog \nmay form this night as well, although confidence remains very low in \nthis. Regardless, it will be our first properly cold night of the \nseason as highs recover Sunday afternoon near the low 60s as surface \nSW flow returns.\n\n===== Mostly Quiet Week Ahead =====\n\nLooking ahead towards next week, the reinforcing NW flow begins to \nweaken a bit, but also remain close enough overhead to give us a \nshot of some glancing rain chances later in the week. Temperatures \nmoderate with surface ridging overhead, highs reaching the low to \nmid 60s on Monday. Long range trends are pointing towards persistent \ntroughing and ridging to our east, allowing the general flow to \nbecome zonal by Tuesday and Wednesday. Without any meaningful \npattern changes, our weather won't change much. We'll remain dry \nwith highs in the mid to upper 60s. Thursday-Friday rain chances \nbegin to creep up some in relation to a weak signal of some \nshortwave disturbances riding down on the nearby troughing that will \nbe centered over eastern Canada. Any shortwave might be bolstered \nwith a jet streak over the central US. However, moisture will be \nlacking as most ensemble guidance keeps rain totals low. There's \nonly a 10-20% of seeing more than 0.25\" of rain across our CWA for \nThursday-Friday. However, if a digging jet streak does occur, cooler \nair will be reinforced for later next week, keeping highs in the 50s.",
